---
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
---

<Docs
	page="adopting"
	title="Adopting squill"
	lede="Bringing squill into a codebase that already has SQL in it: a look first, one reformatting commit, then CI."
>
	<section>
		<h2>1. Write a config</h2>
		<Code lang="sh" code={`squill init`} />
		<p>
			<code>init</code> walks the project the way <code>fmt</code> would and
			lists the languages squill has grammars for, checking the ones where it
			already finds SQL. Then it asks which dialect each is in, and which
			placeholder styles your queries use &mdash; <code>@name</code>,{" "}
			<code>?</code>, <code>:name</code>, <code>%s</code>. Nothing is checked
			for that last one: it depends on your driver, not your language, and the{" "}
			<a href="docs/recipes/rust/">recipes</a> say which each library needs. It
			writes a <code>squill.toml</code> (<code>--yaml</code> for{" "}
			<code>squill.yaml</code>), which is yours to edit from there &mdash; see{" "}
			<a href="docs/configuration/">configuration</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>2. Freeze what has shipped</h2>
		<p>
			Migrations that have already run are the one thing you may not want
			rewritten: some tools checksum them, and every tool&rsquo;s history is
			easier to read when an old migration stays as it ran. Mark them{" "}
			<a href="docs/frozen/">frozen</a>, and squill formats a migration only
			while it&rsquo;s new, on the branch that adds it:
		</p>
		<Code lang="toml" file="squill.toml" code={`frozen = ["migrations/**"]`} />
		<p>
			Migration tools&rsquo; directives &mdash; goose&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>-- +goose StatementBegin</code>, dbmate&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>-- migrate:up</code>, sqlc&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>-- name: GetUser :one</code> &mdash; are comments, and squill never
			moves a comment off its line or past a statement, so new migrations and
			query files format safely.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>3. See what squill would do</h2>
		<Code lang="sh" code={`squill fmt --check .`} />
		<p>
			<code>--check</code> writes nothing: it prints a diff for each file that
			would change, and a line on stderr for everything squill would leave alone
			&mdash; a statement it can&rsquo;t parse, an embedded string it
			can&rsquo;t safely rewrite. That second list is the one to read. Most of
			it comes from a few causes:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Placeholders.</strong> <code>did not parse</code> at a{" "}
				<code>?</code>, <code>@</code>, <code>:</code>, or <code>%</code> means
				the query uses a placeholder style that isn&rsquo;t turned on. Set the
				matching <code>*-params</code> key in the rule.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The dialect.</strong> SQLite code read as Postgres, or the other
				way round. Set <code>dialect</code> in the rule that covers it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Strings squill can&rsquo;t see.</strong> A query built up from
				pieces, or passed through a helper the default query doesn&rsquo;t know.{" "}
				<code>squill locate</code> lists every string the rules find, so a
				missing one is a query to <a href="docs/embedded/#queries">extend</a>.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			What&rsquo;s left is SQL squill doesn&rsquo;t parse yet. It passes through
			exactly as written, so it&rsquo;s safe to leave &mdash; and worth{" "}
			<a href="https://github.com/aslilac/squill/issues">reporting</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>4. Reformat in one commit</h2>
		<Code
			lang="sh"
			code={`squill fmt .
git commit -am "Format SQL with squill"`}
		/>
		<p>
			Keep the reformat in a commit of its own, with nothing else in it, so
			it&rsquo;s easy to review and easy to skip: squill only changes layout,
			keyword case, identifier quoting, and the final <code>;</code>, and turns
			a multi-line Rust or Go string into a raw one. To keep{" "}
			<code>git blame</code> pointing at the commits that wrote each line, list
			it in <code>.git-blame-ignore-revs</code>:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="sh"
			code={`git rev-parse HEAD >> .git-blame-ignore-revs
git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs`}
		/>
		<p>
			GitHub reads that file on its own; locally, each clone needs the{" "}
			<code>git config</code> line once.
		</p>
		<p>
			Branches that were open across the reformat will conflict wherever they
			touched SQL. The quickest way through is to run <code>squill fmt .</code>{" "}
			on the branch before merging trunk into it: with both sides formatted, the
			conflicts that remain are real ones.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>5. Keep it formatted</h2>
		<p>In CI:</p>
		<Code lang="sh" code={`squill fmt --check --locked .`} />
		<p>
			<code>--locked</code> fails on a grammar URL that isn&rsquo;t in{" "}
			<code>squill.lock</code>, rather than downloading one nobody has reviewed.
			Once the list of things squill leaves alone is down to what you&rsquo;ve
			decided to live with, add <code>--strict</code> too, so new SQL that
			squill can&rsquo;t format fails the build instead of slipping by. See{" "}
			<a href="docs/cli/#exit-codes">exit codes</a>.
		</p>
		<p>
			And in your editor, format on save &mdash; see{" "}
			<a href="docs/editors/">editors</a>.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
